<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §01 -->
<section id="s-collision" data-code="collision">
<div class="section-no">01 — Multi-Modal Collision</div>
<h2>The Problem: Parallel Drafters Predict in Isolation</h2>

<p>Speculative decoding accelerates inference with a simple trick: a lightweight draft model proposes <em>γ</em> tokens, the large target model verifies them all in a single forward pass, and accepted tokens come for free. Speed hinges on how many tokens are accepted per round — the accepted length τ.</p>

<p>Two architectures dominate modern drafters. <strong>Autoregressive drafters</strong> (Eagle3) generate tokens one-at-a-time, conditioning each on all previous. High quality, but drafting latency grows linearly with block size — so you're forced into shallow, narrow architectures. <strong>Parallel drafters</strong> (DFlash) generate all γ tokens in one forward pass, so drafting latency is essentially O(1) regardless of block size. Deep, wide networks become affordable.</p>

<p>The catch for parallel drafters: every position is predicted <em>independently</em>. The model marginalizes over all possible predecessors rather than conditioning on the one actually sampled. When two completions are both valid — say "of <strong>course</strong>" and "no <strong>problem</strong>" — the first-position prediction might sample "of" while the second-position prediction, unaware of that choice, produces "problem." Result: "of problem" — a multi-modal collision.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">multi-modal collision — parallel vs semi-AR draft</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to replay</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="collision-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;cursor:pointer"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The animation above shows the core problem: when the context supports two completions ("of course" / "no problem"), a parallel drafter produces an incoherent combination because it can't see what position 1 chose. DSpark's Markov head reads the sampled token at position 1 before committing position 2 — resolving the collision every time.</p>

<p>This acceptance decay isn't just a theoretical concern. In production, chat prompts sustain conditional acceptance rates that drop from 0.72 at position 1 to 0.63 at position 7 for DFlash — wasting nearly half the draft block on tokens that will be rejected. On longer blocks (γ = 15), this decay compounds to a 30% gap over DSpark.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §02 -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">02 — Semi-Autoregressive Architecture</div>
<h2>The Fix: A Lightweight Sequential Head on Top of a Parallel Backbone</h2>

<p>DSpark's core idea is elegant: <em>keep the expensive part parallel, make the cheap part sequential.</em> The parallel backbone (DFlash) runs one forward pass over the full block, producing hidden states h₁…hγ and base logits U₁…Uγ. Then a lightweight Markov head runs a tiny sequential loop — one step per draft token — to inject dependency.</p>

<h3>The Parallel Stage</h3>
<p>DSpark reuses DFlash's parallel backbone with one minor change: instead of taking an anchor token + γ mask tokens and predicting only the masked positions, DSpark treats the anchor itself as the first prediction position. This means γ input tokens produce γ draft logits — same compute, one extra prediction.</p>

<h3>The Markov Head</h3>
<p>The Markov head is a low-rank transition matrix approximated as B = W₁W₂, where W₁ ∈ ℝ<sup>V×r</sup> and W₂ ∈ ℝ<sup>r×V</sup> with rank r = 256. Given the previous sampled token x<sub>k−1</sub>, the transition bias for position k is just:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Markov transition bias</div>
  <span class="math-var">B</span>(x<sub>k−1</sub>, ·) = W₁[x<sub>k−1</sub>] · W₂  ∈ ℝ<sup>V</sup><br>
  <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>k</sub>(v | x<sub>0</sub>, x<sub>&lt;k</sub>) = softmax( U<sub>k</sub>(v) + B(x<sub>k−1</sub>, v) )<br>
  <span class="math-comment">— W₁ is an embedding lookup, W₂ is a logit projection. Rank 256 keeps it tiny.</span>
</div>

<p>The sequential loop is O(γ) with a tiny constant: W₁[x<sub>k−1</sub>] is one embedding lookup, and the bias addition is a matrix-vector product. At batch size 128, this adds only 0.2–1.3% overhead to the full round latency while lifting accepted length by up to 30%.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">DSpark architecture — click through the decoding cycle</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to advance</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="arch-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target generates anchor token D</div>
          <div class="step-desc">One full target-model forward pass on prompt A·B·C produces token D. D becomes the anchor for this draft cycle.</div>
          <div class="step-code">x_anchor = target_model(prompt)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Parallel backbone: one pass → γ logits + hidden states</div>
          <div class="step-desc">DFlash backbone takes D + γ−1 mask tokens, produces base logits U₁…Uγ and hidden states h₁…hγ in a single forward pass. KV injection from target layers grounds the draft in context.</div>
          <div class="step-code">U, H, C = parallel_backbone(x_anchor)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Markov head: sequential loop → adjusted draft tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">For k=1…γ: sample x_k from softmax(U_k + W₁[x_&#123;k−1&#125;]·W₂). Each position conditions on the previously sampled token — eliminating multi-modal collision.</div>
          <div class="step-code">for k in range(gamma):
    bias = W1[x[k-1]] @ W2
    x[k] = sample(U[k] + bias)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Confidence head prunes the draft block</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Confidence scores c₁…cγ are estimated from h_k and W₁[x_&#123;k−1&#125;]. The hardware-aware scheduler computes the optimal prefix length based on system load and truncates low-confidence tails.</div>
          <div class="step-code">c = sigmoid(w @ concat(H[k], W1[x[k-1]]))
ell = scheduler.select_prefix(c, load)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target verifies the scheduled prefix</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Target model verifies x₁…x_ℓ* in one pass. Accepted tokens come for free; the first rejection at position k redirects from k onward. Next cycle starts with the last accepted token as anchor.</div>
          <div class="step-code">accepted = target_verify(x_draft[:ell_star])
new_anchor = accepted[-1]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
