      <button id="arch-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Why 2 Layers Beat 5</h3>
<p>Adding the Markov head is so efficient that a <strong>2-layer DSpark outperforms a 5-layer DFlash</strong> across all domains. Injecting sequential dependency offers a dramatically better accuracy-parameter trade-off than simply stacking more parallel layers. The first-to-second-layer gain is the steepest; beyond 5 layers the marginal returns plateau.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §03 -->
<section id="s-sched" data-code="sched">
<div class="section-no">03 — Confidence-Scheduled Verification</div>
<h2>Verify Smarter: Route Compute Only to High-Confidence Tokens</h2>

<p>Even with a better drafter, blindly verifying the full block wastes compute. DSpark's second innovation is a confidence head that estimates per-position acceptance probability, paired with a hardware-aware scheduler that picks the optimal verification length for the current system load.</p>

<h3>Confidence Head</h3>
<p>A lightweight linear layer + sigmoid estimates c_k — the conditional probability that token k will be accepted, given all prior tokens were accepted. It's supervised against the analytical acceptance rate 1 − ½‖p<sup>d</sup><sub>k</sub> − p<sup>t</sup><sub>k</sub>‖₁ (the total variation distance between draft and target distributions).</p>

<p>Raw neural estimates are overconfident, so DSpark applies <strong>Sequential Temperature Scaling (STS)</strong>: calibrate positions left-to-right, fixing earlier temperatures, minimizing ECE of the cumulative acceptance product at each step. This reduces average ECE from 3–8% to ~1% — critical for the scheduler to reason accurately about prefix survival probabilities.</p>

<h3>Hardware-Aware Scheduler</h3>
<p>The key insight: the cost of verifying an extra token depends entirely on system load. Under light load, even a token likely to be rejected is nearly free to verify. Under heavy load, that same token occupies batch capacity that could serve another user.</p>

<p>The scheduler maximizes expected system-level throughput by solving: "given current engine load and confidence estimates, what prefix length ℓ* maximizes tokens accepted per GPU-second?" At low concurrency, ℓ* = γ (verify everything). As batch pressure rises, ℓ* shrinks, dropping low-confidence tails first.</p>

