<p>In offline threshold sweeps on Qwen3-4B chat data, raising the confidence threshold from 0 to 0.7 increases the overall acceptance rate from 45.7% to 95.7% — a 2× improvement — while dramatically reducing the verified token count. On structured code tasks, the effect is milder (acceptance 67.6% → 92.0%) because code naturally sustains higher acceptance throughout the block.</p>

<h3>Asynchronous Deployment in ZOS</h3>
<p>Production deployment required one more trick: ZOS (Zero-Overhead Scheduling) needs to know the next batch size <em>before</em> the current step completes. DSpark approximates the scheduling signal using confidence estimates from 2 steps prior — a causal barrier that prevents information leakage while enabling seamless CUDA graph replay. Latency cost: zero.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §04 -->
<section id="s-poswise" data-code="poswise">
<div class="section-no">04 — Position-Wise Analysis</div>
<h2>Capacity First, Coherence Throughout</h2>

<p>DSpark's advantage has a clear structural explanation. The interactive chart below shows per-position conditional acceptance rates for Eagle3, DFlash, and DSpark on the Qwen3-4B target. "Conditional" means: given all earlier positions were accepted, what fraction of position-k tokens are also accepted? This removes the confound of prefix errors and measures pure per-position quality.</p>

