<h3>The Capacity Advantage at Position 1</h3>
<p>At position 1, both DFlash and DSpark start much higher than Eagle3. Why? Parallel drafters can afford deep, wide architectures (O(1) draft latency), while Eagle3 must stay shallow (O(γ) latency). This structural capacity gap produces a meaningful accuracy margin at position 1 — and because speculative decoding is a strict prefix-matching process, the first token has the highest leverage. A rejection at position 1 invalidates the entire block.</p>

<p>The numbers: on Chat, DFlash starts at 0.72 vs Eagle3 at 0.53 — a 0.19 gap that cascades through the entire block. On Math, DFlash starts at 0.88 vs Eagle3 at 0.81.</p>

<h3>The Coherence Advantage at Positions 2–7</h3>
<p>But here's the twist: Eagle3 <em>improves</em> down the block. On Chat, Eagle3 rises from 0.53 to 0.74 by position 7, while DFlash drops from 0.72 to 0.63. Eagle3's autoregressive conditioning means later tokens become <em>easier</em> given earlier context — once it commits to "of course," position 2 is nearly certain. DFlash's independence means it can't exploit this conditional certainty.</p>

<p>DSpark combines both advantages: it starts high (parallel backbone capacity at position 1) and stays high (Markov head coherence at positions 2–7). On Chat, DSpark maintains ~0.85–0.88 conditional acceptance throughout the full block — neither decaying like DFlash nor starting low like Eagle3.</p>
</section>

